WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.140
In September of 2008, something unheard of happened in the art world.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:12.420
A British artist, Damien Hirst, took 223 pieces of his new work to Sotheby’s auction house

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.320
and sold every single piece.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.460
I’ll start the bidding here at £2,500,000.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:25.300
It was a 2 day event, and the total sale was about $200 million.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.400
It broke the record for single artist auction
of $20 million back in 1993.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:35.820
Hirst’s work&nbsp;included things like this
zebra, this unicorn, and this painting made

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:36.830
from butterflies.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:41.000
So how did he –&nbsp;how do artists&nbsp;– do this?

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.399
Well for the most part, the artists aren't
the ones behind it.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:48.680
Okay, so obviously money isn’t the only value

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:54.360
that defines a work of art, and&nbsp;who knows how history will remember this unicorn.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.400
But order to sell a million-dollar artwork, you need strong market value– and you

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.760
need extremely high demand.
And a ton of work goes into creating that.

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.520
As Don Thompson describes in his book, the formula for art pricing goes something like this.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.060
The bigger the work, generally the more expensive it is.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:17.720
But, the biggest variable is the reputation
of the artist. Sometimes you’re world-famous,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.300
and sometimes you’re not.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:19.940
What?

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.520
But when a new artist steps into the art market,

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:26.240
the reputation of the artist heavily relies
on the name of the dealer.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.560
This shark by Damien Hirst is a good example. Hirst first began working with an art industry

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.440
giant, Charles Saatchi, in the 1990s.
Saatchi commissioned Hirst to make anything

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.260
he wanted for £50,000 after seeing this cow’s head at a show shortly after Hirst’s graduation

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.639
in 1990. Hirst bought a shark for £6000 from an Australian

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.259
fisherman and created this, injecting it with tons of formaldehyde.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:56.520
Later in 2004, it was sold for $12 million to a billionaire hedge fund manager,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.319
Steve Cohen.
It was roughly 130 times the original price

00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:05.119
but it makes sense considering Saatchi’s reputation. And it makes more sense when you think about

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:11.219
how it was bought –&nbsp;dealers can use selective information to get potential buyers to pay more.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.000
Hirst’s huge auction I mentioned before…

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.160
For dealer-sold work, everything is private,
including the prices, which gives dealers

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.410
the upper hand in pricing.
In 1988, New York City tried to ban this by

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.800
reinforcing the Truth in Pricing law, and
galleries fought back HARD, paying fines and

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.430
protesting saying that showing prices will
be “getting in the way of the enjoyment

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.950
of the exhibition.”
By keeping the price private, art dealers

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.990
can rely more on their reputation to make
the artwork feel more valuable to the buyer.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:49.820
Outside this equation, the basic laws of economics also apply. The next step of operations for

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:55.380
the dealer is creating scarcity.
In 1999, when Jenny Saville, a new emerging

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:01.080
British artist became affiliated with Charles Saatchi, he convinced her to cut her work down to only

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.520
6 paintings per year. He sold them for $100,000 each.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.720
So what does this all result to?
According to Artnet, the estimated size of

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.910
the art market was $64 billion in 2015.
And&nbsp;market is growing outside of traditional

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:20.560
sales of galleries and auction houses.
This chart shows the art world might be learning

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.010
the lesson Saatchi taught Jenny Savile –the total value of the art that’s being sold

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.060
is growing faster than the number of pieces. Sell less of it, for more.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:33.561
But to sell that million-dollar artwork, you’ll need reputation bigger than Hirst’s, or

00:03:33.561 --> 00:03:37.100
Charles Saatchi’s.
The dealer model still dominates the fine

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:42.260
art world, but for the rest of us, selling
art online has never been easier. The prices

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.580
are open and it’s accessible for a broad
group of people.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.640
And for one thing, now you know where to start:

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.020
think big.

